We test this on 13 binary item-by-model matrices (seven from the Open LLM Leaderboard, six from HELM), under three splits of models into sources and targets (random models, held-out model families, and targets above every source), with seven designs, three predictors and their model, plug-in and difference estimators (Section~\ref{sec:setup}).
The primary endpoint, fixed beforehand, is the RMSE relative to uniform sampling at $n=100$, with stratified sampling as the second baseline.
Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item a controlled comparison of item-selection designs and estimators, so that a gain can be attributed to the design, to the estimator or to both (Section~\ref{sec:res-ablation});
\item a measurement of how the error of source-fitted predictors depends on the distance of the target above the sources, split into bias and variance (Section~\ref{sec:res-bias});
\item a reference for how much a predictor can help, from the fraction of item variance it explains (Section~\ref{sec:res-ceiling});
\item the finding that error reductions do not always carry over to the ordering of models (Section~\ref{sec:res-order}).
\end{itemize}

The answer is conditional.
When targets resemble the sources, the source-aided classes chosen without looking at the target benchmark's outcomes reach $0.65$--$0.79$ of the RMSE of uniform sampling at $n=100$ and $0.80$--$0.94$ of that of stratified sampling, model-based and difference-corrected methods being the best.
When every target is stronger than every source, our IRT and kNN predictors are worse than uniform sampling ($1.96$ and $2.42$ times its RMSE) because they compress their estimates toward the source mean (the published tinyBenchmarks IRT estimator, run on six benchmarks, is not: $0.88$; Appendix~\ref{app:public}), by $5$ to $10$ points for targets one to three standard deviations above the sources; a difference correction reduces the bias without removing it, and what remains over stratified sampling is at most a tenth of its error, delivered by a balanced design paired with calibrated regression and by no other class.
Anchor selection with an IRT plug-in estimate (a tinyBenchmarks-style method, our re-implementation) is not clearly better than uniform sampling in interpolation or lineage ($0.95$ and $1.03$).
The code, the result tables and the scripts that draw every figure and table are released with the paper (Appendix~\ref{app:repro}).
